<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 3.1 - Invertibility - Part 2</title>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="1in" xml:id="sec3-1-2-matrixA">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose
                    <md>
                        A = \left[\begin{array}{rrr} 1\amp0\amp-2\\ 1\amp-1\amp0 \\ -1\amp2\amp1  \end{array}\right].
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    If <m>B =  \left[\bvec_1 \ \bvec_2 \  \bvec_3 \right]</m> is
                    the inverse of <m>A</m>, we could solve the following three
                    equations separately to find the columns of <m>B</m>:
                    <md>
                        A\bvec_1 = \evec_1=\threevec100, \ \ \  A\bvec_2 = \evec_2=\threevec010, \ \ \ A\bvec_3 = \evec_3=\threevec001.
                    </md>
                    But, we may as well solve them at the same time.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        To do this, build the augmented matrix
                        <md>
                            \left[
      \begin{array}{c|c}
        A \amp I \\
      \end{array}
    \right]
                        </md>
                        and find the reduced row echelon form.
                        How does this produce <m>B =
    A^{-1}</m>?
                        Note: In Sage, you may create the <m>3\times3</m>
                        identity matrix by <c>identity_matrix(3)</c>.
                        You may augment <m>A</m> using
                        <c>A.augment(identity_matrix(3))</c>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        For each of the individual systems of the three
                        equations above, we would use the same row operations
                        every time.
                        If we augment with the identity matrix, the first column
                        corresponds to solving the system
                        <m>A\bvec_1 = \evec_1</m>, the second column to solving
                        the system <m>A\bvec_2 = \evec_2</m>, and the third with
                        <m>A\bvec_3 = \evec_3</m>.
                        Since all use the same row operations, we can solve them
                        together, where the results augmented matrix will hold
                        the three columns of <m>B</m>, namely,
                        <m>\left[\bvec_1 \ \bvec_2 \  \bvec_3 \right].</m>
                        Consider the Sage code and output below.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,-2,
                1,-1,0,
                -1,2,1])
C = A.augment(identity_matrix(3))
C.rref()
                        </input>
                        <output>
[1 0 0  1/3 4/3 2/3]
[0 1 0  1/3 1/3 2/3]
[0 0 1 -1/3 2/3 1/3]
                        </output>
                    </sage>

                    <p>
                        Therefore,
                        <m>A^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix}
                    \frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{4}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \\
                    \frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \\
                    -\frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{2}{3} \amp \frac{1}{3} \\
                    \end{bmatrix}.
                    </m>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Use <m>B= A^{-1}</m> to solve the equation
                        <m>A\xvec = \threevec723</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        Multiply both sides by <m>B</m> such that
                        <m>{\xvec = B\threevec{7}{2}{3} = 7\threevec{\frac{1}{3}}{\frac{1}{3}}{-\frac{1}{3}} + 2\threevec{\frac{4}{3}}{\frac{1}{3}}{\frac{2}{3}} + 3\threevec{\frac{2}{3}}{\frac{2}{3}}{\frac{1}{3}} = \threevec{7}{5}{0}}</m>.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,-2,
                1,-1,0,
                -1,2,1])
C = A.augment(identity_matrix(3))
D = C.rref()
B = D.matrix_from_columns([3,4,5]) # takes the columns corresponding to the inverse
x = vector([7,2,3])
B*x
                        </input>
                        <output>
(7, 5, 0)
                        </output>
                    </sage>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the matrix
                    <md>
                        B =
  \left[
    \begin{array}{rrr}
      -3 \amp 2 \amp -2 \\
      1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
      1 \amp 2 \amp -2 \\
    \end{array}
  \right].
                    </md>
                    Use Sage for this question!
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Explain why the matrix <m>B</m> is invertible.
                        (Think about row reducing.)
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        <m>B</m> is invertible since it row reduces to the
                        identity matrix.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
B = matrix(3,3,[-3, 2, -2,
                1, 0, 3,
                1, 2, -2])
B.rref()
                        </input>
                        <output>
[1 0 0]
[0 1 0]
[0 0 1]
                        </output>
                    </sage>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find the inverse <m>B^{-1}</m>.
                        Note: In Sage, you may find the inverse of a matrix
                        <c>B</c> using <c>B.inverse()</c> or <c>B^(-1)</c>.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        The inverse is
                        <m>B^{-1} = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
                    -\frac{1}{4} \amp 0 \amp \frac{1}{4} \\
                    \frac{5}{24} \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{7}{24} \\
                    \frac{1}{12} \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{12} \\
                    \end{array}\right].</m>
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
B = matrix(3,3,[-3, 2, -2,
                1, 0, 3,
                1, 2, -2])
B.inverse()
                        </input>
                        <output>
[-1/4    0   1/4]
[5/24  1/3  7/24]
[1/12  1/3 -1/12]
                        </output>
                    </sage>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Using <m>A</m> from <xref ref="sec3-1-2-matrixA"/> and
                        <m>B</m> from this problem, is <m>AB</m> invertible?
                        If so, what is its inverse?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        Yes, the matrix <m>AB</m> row reduces to <m>I_3</m>, so
                        <m>AB</m> is invertible.
                        The inverse is
                        <m>(AB)^{-1} = \left[\begin{array}{rrr}
                    -\frac{1}{6} \amp -\frac{1}{6} \amp -\frac{1}{12} \\
                    \frac{1}{12} \amp \frac{7}{12} \amp \frac{11}{24} \\
                    \frac{1}{6} \amp \frac{1}{6} \amp \frac{1}{4} \\
                    \end{array}\right].</m>
                        We can find this using Sage - see below.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,-2,
                1,-1,0,
                -1,2,1])
B = matrix(3,3,[-3, 2, -2,
                1, 0, 3,
                1, 2, -2])
D = A*B
D.inverse()
                        </input>
                        <output>
[-1/6  -1/6   -1/12]
[1/12  7/12   11/24]
[ 1/6   1/6     1/4]
                        </output>
                    </sage>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find <m>A^{-1}B^{-1}</m> and <m>B^{-1}A^{-1}</m>.
                        Which one is the same as <m>(AB)^{-1}</m>?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        Using <m>B^{-1}</m> found above and <m>A^{-1}</m> found
                        in <xref ref="sec3-1-2-matrixA"/>, we have that
                        <m>A^{-1}B^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 
                    \frac{1}{4} \amp \frac{2}{3} \amp \frac{5}{12} \\
                    \frac{1}{24} \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{1}{8} \\
                    \frac{1}{4} \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp \frac{1}{12} \\
                     \end{bmatrix}.</m>
                        See below for confirmation using Sage.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,-2,
                1,-1,0,
                -1,2,1])
B = matrix(3,3,[-3, 2, -2,
                1, 0, 3,
                1, 2, -2])
print(A.inverse()*B.inverse())
                        </input>
                        <output>
[ 1/4  2/3  5/12]
[1/24  1/3   1/8]
[ 1/4  1/3  1/12]
                        </output>
                    </sage>

                    <p>
                        Secondly, we have that
                        <m>B^{-1}A^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix} 
                    -\frac{1}{6} \amp -\frac{1}{6} \amp -\frac{1}{12} \\
                                        \frac{1}{12} \amp \frac{7}{12} \amp \frac{11}{24} \\
                                        \frac{1}{6} \amp \frac{1}{6} \amp \frac{1}{4} \\
                     \end{bmatrix}.</m>
                        See below for confirmation using Sage.
                    </p>
                    <sage>
                        <input>
A = matrix(3,3,[1,0,-2,
                1,-1,0,
                -1,2,1])
B = matrix(3,3,[-3, 2, -2,
                1, 0, 3,
                1, 2, -2])
print(B.inverse()*A.inverse())
                        </input>
                        <output>
[-1/6  -1/6   -1/12]
[1/12  7/12   11/24]
[ 1/6   1/6     1/4]
                        </output>
                    </sage>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise workspace="1.5in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible
                    <m>n\times n</m> matrices.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1.5in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Explain why <m>(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}</m> by
                        simplifying
                        <md>
                            (AB)(B^{-1}A^{-1})
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        <md>
                            <mrow>
                    (AB)(B^{-1}A^{-1}) \amp = \amp A(BB^{-1})A^{-1} 
                    </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                    \amp = \amp A(I_n)A^{-1} 
                    </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                    \amp = \amp AA^{-1} 
                    </mrow>
                            <mrow>
                    \amp = \amp I_n.
                    </mrow>
                        </md>
                        This means that <m>AB</m> and <m>B^{-1}A^{-1}</m> are
                        inverses of each other.
                        Thus, <m>{(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}.}</m>
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Explain why the product of two invertible
                        <m>n\times n</m> matrices is always invertible.
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        Since we know that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible,
                        the inverses <m>A^{-1}</m> and <m>B^{-1}</m> must exist.
                        Therefore, their product <m>B^{-1}A^{-1}</m> also
                        exists.
                        Since <m>{B^{-1}A^{-1} = (AB)^{-1}}</m>, it must be true
                        that the inverse of the product of two invertible
                        matrices always exists.
                        Thus, this product is always invertible.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>A</m> is a <m>7\times7</m> matrix whose
                    columns are linearly independent.
                    Can you guarantee that <m>A</m> is invertible?
                    Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="16-solutions">
                <p>
                    Yes, there is a pivot in every column since they are
                    independent and <m>A</m> is square, so there is also a pivot
                    in every row.
                    This means that <m>A</m> row reduces to <m>I_7</m> and is
                    thus invertible.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    <em>If time:</em> A matrix <m>A</m> is lower triangular if
                    all its entries above the diagonal are zero.
                    A matrix <m>A</m> is upper triangular if all of its entries
                    below the diagonal are zero.
                    E.g., <m>L</m> is lower triangular and <m>U</m> is upper
                    triangular:
                    <md>
                        L = \begin{bmatrix}
	* \amp0 \amp0\amp0\\
	* \amp* \amp0 \amp0\\
	* \amp* \amp* \amp0\\
	* \amp* \amp* \amp*
	\end{bmatrix}	\qquad
	U = \begin{bmatrix}
	* \amp* \amp* \amp*\\
	0 \amp* \amp* \amp*\\
	0 \amp0 \amp* \amp*\\
	0 \amp0 \amp0 \amp*
	\end{bmatrix}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Which of the two triangular matrices below are
                        invertible?
                        <md>
                            \left[\begin{array}{ccc}1\amp0\amp7\\0\amp-2\amp5\\0\amp0\amp-9  \end{array}\right], ~~~  \left[\begin{array}{ccc} 1\amp0\amp7\\ 0\amp-2\amp5\\0\amp0\amp0 \end{array}\right]
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        <m>\left[\begin{array}{ccc}1\amp0\amp7\\0\amp-2\amp5\\0\amp0\amp-9  \end{array}\right]</m>
                        is invertible because there is a pivot along the main
                        diagonal such that after continued Gaussian Elimination
                        the reduced row echelon form would be <m>I_3</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        <m>\left[\begin{array}{ccc} 1\amp0\amp7\\ 0\amp-2\amp5\\0\amp0\amp0 \end{array}\right]</m>
                        is not invertible because there is no pivot in the last
                        column.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Without doing any row reduction, can you figure out how
                        to tell if a square lower or upper triangular matrix is
                        invertible?
                    </p>
                </statement>

                <solution component="16-solutions">
                    <p>
                        There will be a pivot in every column and row if all the
                        entries on the diagonal are nonzero.
                        This is true for both square lower and upper triangular
                        matrices.
                        Therefore, to be invertible the square lower or upper
                        triangular matrix must have all nonzero entries on the
                        diagonal.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <outcomes>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Make sure you can answer the following questions:
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <ul>
            <!-- <li><p>Given a matrix, how can one use row reduction to tell if the matrix is invertible?</p></li>
      <li><p>How can we use the inverse of a matrix <m>A</m> to solve <m>Ax=b</m>.</p></li> -->
            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we find the inverse of a matrix using row reduction?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How do we find the inverse of a matrix using Sage?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How can we determine if a matrix is invertible using its
                    reduced row echelon form?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is <m>(AB)^{-1}</m> in terms of <m>A^{-1}</m> and
                    <m>B^{-1}</m>?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>